1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,623
This is a recording of one
real run, start to finish.

2
00:00:03,623 --> 00:00:08,300
The source is iDempiere, an open source E
R P on Oracle, with nine hundred tables,

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,779
twenty years of migration scripts
and a large Java code base.

4
00:00:11,779 --> 00:00:16,456
We create a new project in the Ekos console
and take it through the seven steps of

5
00:00:16,456 --> 00:00:17,711
the operator workflow.

6
00:00:17,711 --> 00:00:18,852
Analyse the sources.

7
00:00:18,852 --> 00:00:20,392
Generate the documentation.

8
00:00:20,392 --> 00:00:22,502
Review and sign off the source model.

9
00:00:22,502 --> 00:00:24,156
Describe the reports we want.

10
00:00:24,156 --> 00:00:26,038
Approve how each table is loaded.

11
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:27,407
Build bronze and silver.

12
00:00:27,407 --> 00:00:30,886
And build the gold marts, each
one reconciled against silver.

13
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:35,677
Nothing here is staged: every screen is
from this run, and every number we say is on

14
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:36,076
screen.

15
00:00:36,076 --> 00:00:40,753
Where something went wrong during the
recording, we say so, and we show how it was

16
00:00:40,753 --> 00:00:41,096
fixed.

17
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,358
First, what we are dealing with.

18
00:00:44,358 --> 00:00:48,644
This map is how Ekos compiled the iDempiere
code base: the twenty largest path

19
00:00:48,644 --> 00:00:52,986
groups, from the base module with three
thousand and eleven objects to the user

20
00:00:52,986 --> 00:00:57,601
interface and the migration scripts, linked
by how often their files change together

21
00:00:57,601 --> 00:00:58,645
in the git history.

22
00:00:58,645 --> 00:01:02,986
The second picture is the part of the
database this lakehouse is built for: the

23
00:01:02,986 --> 00:01:07,437
order to invoice slice, thirty three tables
and their declared foreign keys, with

24
00:01:07,437 --> 00:01:10,954
orders, order lines, invoices and
business partners highlighted.

25
00:01:10,954 --> 00:01:14,086
Both pictures are drawn from
compiled facts, not by hand.

26
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:16,120
Step one.

27
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:20,687
The Sources page holds the code checkout,
the documentation site and the Oracle

28
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:23,173
database, read through a read only account.

29
00:01:23,173 --> 00:01:25,832
Testing the connection reads the live catalog.

30
00:01:25,832 --> 00:01:27,740
Then one click: Init and compile.

31
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:32,481
Ekos reads the code, the scripts, the
documents and the catalog, and compiles them

32
00:01:32,481 --> 00:01:37,106
into one ledger, with no language model
involved: the run page counts zero model

33
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:37,452
calls.

34
00:01:37,452 --> 00:01:42,019
On this laptop the whole analysis took
forty nine minutes, most of it the final

35
00:01:42,019 --> 00:01:44,390
commit, because the machine was swapping.

36
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:49,188
Afterwards the census agrees with what was
compiled: nine hundred and three tables,

37
00:01:49,188 --> 00:01:52,946
one hundred and sixty views and
sixty two routines on both sides.

38
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:57,686
The ledger now holds six hundred and seventy
five thousand and thirty two entries.

39
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,541
Step two: documentation.

40
00:02:00,541 --> 00:02:05,124
Generate writes the estate as wiki pages,
straight from the ledger: the data model

41
00:02:05,124 --> 00:02:09,315
by area, a page per table and routine,
the schema history and the findings.

42
00:02:09,315 --> 00:02:11,215
No data value ever reaches a page.

43
00:02:11,215 --> 00:02:15,518
Preview publish shows what would change
in the documentation store, here this

44
00:02:15,518 --> 00:02:18,368
repository's GitHub wiki,
without sending anything.

45
00:02:18,368 --> 00:02:22,895
Once the review in step three narrowed
the scope, the pages were generated again,

46
00:02:22,895 --> 00:02:25,745
two hundred and thirty nine
of them, and published.

47
00:02:25,745 --> 00:02:28,260
The step bar now marks documentation as done.

48
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:34,142
Step three is the review, and it is a
person's job; here, a demo operator.

49
00:02:34,142 --> 00:02:38,635
Six subjects: scope, personal data,
conflicts, identity, write behaviour and

50
00:02:38,635 --> 00:02:39,167
glossary.

51
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:39,877
Scope first.

52
00:02:39,877 --> 00:02:43,838
Ekos proposed eight hundred and
forty nine tables as business data.

53
00:02:43,838 --> 00:02:48,627
This lakehouse is for the order to invoice
slice, so one command from the console

54
00:02:48,627 --> 00:02:53,120
puts the other eight hundred and sixteen
tables out of scope, with a reason.

55
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:54,953
The review now shows the slice.

56
00:02:54,953 --> 00:02:59,742
Signing off means typing the subject's
name: the sign off is bound to a digest of

57
00:02:59,742 --> 00:03:04,353
exactly what was shown, and turns stale
by itself if a fact behind it changes.

58
00:03:05,867 --> 00:03:09,115
Inside the slice, seventeen
conflicts block the sign off.

59
00:03:09,115 --> 00:03:13,673
Each one is a column the application's
model calls mandatory, while the database

60
00:03:13,673 --> 00:03:14,356
allows null.

61
00:03:14,356 --> 00:03:18,743
Ekos keeps both claims, with their
sources, and measures the data: here, zero

62
00:03:18,743 --> 00:03:19,826
contradicting rows.

63
00:03:19,826 --> 00:03:22,959
The operator decides that
both are valid, and says why.

64
00:03:22,959 --> 00:03:26,549
One identity question remains:
a git handle and a display name.

65
00:03:26,549 --> 00:03:31,221
The handle is the local part of that
person's commit address, so they are the same

66
00:03:31,221 --> 00:03:31,905
contributor.

67
00:03:31,905 --> 00:03:35,722
Personal data, write behaviour and
the glossary are signed as well.

68
00:03:35,722 --> 00:03:39,710
Six of six subjects signed: steps
four to seven are no longer blocked.

69
00:03:41,210 --> 00:03:45,578
We continue with step five, not four,
and that is a finding of this recording:

70
00:03:45,578 --> 00:03:50,170
requirements resolve against the deployed
plan, so the plan has to be approved and

71
00:03:50,170 --> 00:03:53,585
generated first, even though
the step bar suggests step four.

72
00:03:53,585 --> 00:03:57,785
The plan says, for each of the thirty
three tables, how it lands in bronze.

73
00:03:57,785 --> 00:04:02,377
Incremental where the application's code
keeps the updated column, full otherwise;

74
00:04:02,377 --> 00:04:05,233
how deletes are detected;
which columns are masked.

75
00:04:05,233 --> 00:04:07,137
Every decision shows its evidence.

76
00:04:07,137 --> 00:04:11,841
The operator changes one table to a full
load, with a reason, and approves the plan.

77
00:04:11,841 --> 00:04:15,425
Approval binds the plan's digest:
nothing else can be generated.

78
00:04:16,925 --> 00:04:21,779
Generate writes the pipeline: the extraction
SQL, the dbt silver models with their

79
00:04:21,779 --> 00:04:23,377
tests, and the Dagster job.

80
00:04:23,377 --> 00:04:24,206
Now step four.

81
00:04:24,206 --> 00:04:29,001
Two marts are described as Markdown: sales
by month and customer, and invoices by

82
00:04:29,001 --> 00:04:29,356
month.

83
00:04:29,356 --> 00:04:30,540
Every term resolves.

84
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:35,157
The third is written in plain words: net
sales of completed customer orders by

85
00:04:35,157 --> 00:04:38,117
month, product category
and the customer's region.

86
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:40,544
The local model, llama3, drafts the mart.

87
00:04:40,544 --> 00:04:45,457
This is the only language model call in
the whole workflow, and Ekos does not trust

88
00:04:45,457 --> 00:04:48,654
it: it resolves every term
against the approved model.

89
00:04:48,654 --> 00:04:49,541
Two terms fail.

90
00:04:49,541 --> 00:04:54,336
The measure is an expression the resolver
does not accept, and the filter names a

91
00:04:54,336 --> 00:04:55,935
column that does not exist.

92
00:04:57,435 --> 00:05:00,132
Questions go to the operator,
never back to the model.

93
00:05:00,132 --> 00:05:04,228
The operator replaces the measure with the
sum of the order lines' net amount, and

94
00:05:04,228 --> 00:05:06,326
the filter with completed customer orders.

95
00:05:06,326 --> 00:05:10,521
Then Ekos asks a harder question: the
customer's region can be reached from an order

96
00:05:10,521 --> 00:05:14,717
line along many different foreign key paths,
each with a different meaning, and Ekos

97
00:05:14,717 --> 00:05:15,616
will not pick one.

98
00:05:15,616 --> 00:05:19,013
The operator names the path through
the business partner's location.

99
00:05:19,013 --> 00:05:22,909
Every term now resolves, and the change
log keeps each answer and who gave it.

100
00:05:22,909 --> 00:05:24,707
All three requirements are approved.

101
00:05:26,207 --> 00:05:27,176
Run the pipeline.

102
00:05:27,176 --> 00:05:31,622
Dagster loads the thirty three tables
from Oracle into bronze, in Iceberg, and

103
00:05:31,622 --> 00:05:32,933
builds silver with dbt.

104
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:37,607
The run succeeded: thirty six steps in
five minutes and three seconds, sixty eight

105
00:05:37,607 --> 00:05:40,343
assets and eight hundred
and ninety five checks.

106
00:05:40,343 --> 00:05:44,048
Getting here took three launches,
and this is the second finding.

107
00:05:44,048 --> 00:05:48,836
The new project's silver first landed in
the schema an earlier project uses, because

108
00:05:48,836 --> 00:05:52,142
silver and gold schema names
ignored the project's prefix.

109
00:05:52,142 --> 00:05:56,873
We stopped, fixed the product so each
project gets its own silver and gold schemas,

110
00:05:56,873 --> 00:05:58,298
regenerated and reloaded.

111
00:05:58,298 --> 00:06:02,972
Two tables carry data warnings: a document
type and a payment type hold a code the

112
00:06:02,972 --> 00:06:05,309
application's code list does not declare.

113
00:06:06,809 --> 00:06:10,165
Step seven turns each approved
requirement into a gold mart.

114
00:06:10,165 --> 00:06:14,528
Propose compiles the description into a
dbt model on a branch, and checks that

115
00:06:14,528 --> 00:06:16,038
nothing in it was invented.

116
00:06:16,038 --> 00:06:17,884
Merge is the operator's approval.

117
00:06:17,884 --> 00:06:21,016
Build runs only that mart and
its tests through Dagster.

118
00:06:21,016 --> 00:06:25,659
Reconcile reads each measure's total from
the mart and computes the same total from

119
00:06:25,659 --> 00:06:26,050
silver.

120
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,581
Every term is exact, and every total
matches: three hundred and fifty eight point

121
00:06:30,581 --> 00:06:35,279
four zero for net sales by region and
category, three hundred and seventy nine point

122
00:06:35,279 --> 00:06:37,852
nine zero and three orders for sales by month.

123
00:06:37,852 --> 00:06:41,488
All three marts are reconciled,
in the project's own gold schema.

124
00:06:42,988 --> 00:06:47,373
dbt's own lineage graph shows the models:
bronze sources on the left, the silver

125
00:06:47,373 --> 00:06:49,895
models, and the three gold marts on the right.

126
00:06:49,895 --> 00:06:51,758
Column level lineage goes further.

127
00:06:51,758 --> 00:06:56,253
For net sales, the order line's net amount
is cast into bronze, the latest version

128
00:06:56,253 --> 00:06:58,226
is kept in silver, and gold sums it.

129
00:06:58,226 --> 00:07:01,296
Month comes from the order
date, truncated to the month.

130
00:07:01,296 --> 00:07:04,640
Every step is read from the
generated SQL, not drawn by hand.

131
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:09,189
Making this work for Oracle's upper case
names was the third fix of this recording:

132
00:07:09,189 --> 00:07:12,094
the documentation tool
matched names with their case.

133
00:07:13,594 --> 00:07:14,152
To sum up.

134
00:07:14,152 --> 00:07:18,840
One new project, analysed from code, documents
and the live database with zero model

135
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:19,175
calls.

136
00:07:19,175 --> 00:07:22,580
Two hundred and thirty nine
pages of documentation published.

137
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:25,594
A review signed off by a
person, decision by decision.

138
00:07:25,594 --> 00:07:30,058
Three marts described in Markdown or in
plain words, resolved without invention,

139
00:07:30,058 --> 00:07:32,793
built through Dagster and
reconciled to the cent.

140
00:07:32,793 --> 00:07:37,370
What is still open: two code values outside
the application's lists in silver, the

141
00:07:37,370 --> 00:07:41,779
dashboard's growth chart timing out on
this ledger, and the step bar suggesting

142
00:07:41,779 --> 00:07:43,398
requirements before the plan.

143
00:07:43,398 --> 00:07:46,858
Two product defects were found
and fixed during the recording.

144
00:07:46,858 --> 00:07:50,653
Everything is in the repository,
with the run log of this recording.

